{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unspecific-digitiform-earthworm","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context\n\nEstablish the nested `rebar bridge` CLI through a shippable vertical slice. The parent story originally proposed advertising all eight final verbs before their owning stories implemented them. That would expose broken placeholders on `main`. This task therefore registers only the two verbs it owns now: `preview` and `sync`. Later dependency-ordered stories extend the group only when each handler is functional.\n\n`preview` is the read-only path over the existing reconciler dry-run mode. `sync` is the mutating path over the existing live mode. The legacy top-level `reconcile`, `jira-onboard`, `bridge-probe`, and `bridge-fsck` spellings remain unchanged at this boundary.\n\n## Approach\n\n- Add `src/rebar/_cli/_bridge_commands.py` as the nested-group parser and shared reconciler subprocess launcher.\n- Keep `_cli._reconcile` as a compatibility wrapper around that launcher until story `4a44-4abb-ae91-4e51` retires the top-level verb.\n- Route the help-backed `bridge` noun from `src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py`.\n- Register only `preview` and `sync`: preview forces `dry-run`; sync forces `live`.\n- Reject `--mode`, `--filter-local-ids`, and arbitrary trailing arguments so callers cannot override the read/write distinction. Public `--max-changes`, `--only`, `--except`, and `--max-age` remain with `4a44`.\n- Define exact discovery contracts: no nested verb prints group usage; `--help` prints usage; an unknown nested verb fails with the valid choices and never falls through to a same-named top-level command.\n- Regenerate the CLI reference and document only the staged surface that actually ships.\n- Preserve `Mode`, `MODE_CAPS`, the `gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"` round trip, top-level `doctor`, and all legacy bridge spellings.\n\n## Surface parity\n\n- CLI: in scope and exercised through the real `rebar` entrypoint.\n- Library and MCP: out of scope; story `f789-3f9d-8b35-4e0d` owns those surfaces.\n- Automated workflows and scripts: out of scope; story `4a44-4abb-ae91-4e51` owns their migration.\n- Dual direction: both preview/read and sync/write are proven here.\n\n## Testing\n\nUse a real CLI subprocess against a temporary git-backed tracker plus a stateful fake Jira transport built from production-shaped fixtures. A unit-only dispatch test is insufficient because parsing, subprocess wiring, mode selection, and persisted state are contractual.\n\nRED-first oracle:\n- happy path: `rebar bridge preview` returns the real reconciler JSON plan;\n- held out: exact before/after snapshots prove preview performs zero Jira writes and zero ticket or bridge-state writes;\n- held out: sync starts from an unbound remote-only issue, creates the real local/binding mutation, and preserves unrelated event bytes;\n- held out: no-verb, help, unknown verb, top-level-name collision, forbidden flag, phase-gate, doctor, and legacy-command contracts;\n- mutation: routing preview to live must fail the no-write oracle; routing sync to dry-run must fail the persisted-mutation oracle.\n\nThis wrapper adds no new composed store operation, retry loop, or cache, so the shared contention suites remain the responsibility of the reconciler core.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar bridge`, `rebar bridge --help`, `rebar bridge preview --help`, and `rebar bridge sync --help` expose only functional `preview` and `sync` verbs with pinned exit/stream contracts.\n- [ ] An unknown nested verb fails with valid choices and never dispatches a same-named top-level command.\n- [ ] `bridge preview` performs zero Jira writes and zero ticket or bridge-state writes, proven by exact snapshots and the fake transport log.\n- [ ] `bridge sync` performs a persisted mutation from a production-shaped fake Jira fixture while preserving unrelated state.\n- [ ] `--mode`, `--filter-local-ids`, and unknown arguments are rejected on both verbs.\n- [ ] Help text contains none of the prohibited internal vocabulary from the parent story.\n- [ ] `Mode`, `MODE_CAPS`, and `gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"` remain intact and exercised.\n- [ ] Top-level `doctor`, `reconcile`, `jira-onboard`, `bridge-probe`, and `bridge-fsck` behavior remains unchanged.\n- [ ] `docs/cli-reference.md`, `docs/user-guide.md`, and `docs/release-notes.md` describe the staged surface without claiming sibling-owned verbs already ship.\n- [ ] Focused pytest nodes, `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `make test` pass.\n\n## Rollback\n\nThis is additive and changes no persisted schema. Revert the single Gerrit change to remove the nested group while leaving every legacy spelling intact.","id":"b42a-ffc3-deeb-4491","parent_id":"4752-3afb-e8bd-448a","priority":1,"tags":["bridge","cli","ux"],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Add safe bridge noun with functional preview and sync"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786142317990206001,"uuid":"5f897184-7a70-4fb5-a3e4-d0f6deee45b5"}